# Calm-Map Project Rules for AI Agents

## Start all agent chats with an emoji

## Project Overview
Calm-Map is a GIS web application that helps users discover calm, peaceful places in their community. Built as a single-page application using vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript with Leaflet.js for mapping functionality.

## Tech Stack
- **Frontend**: Vanilla HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript (ES6+)
- **Mapping**: Leaflet.js v1.9.4
- **Basemap**: Esri Vector Tile Service
- **Data**: JSON file (`points.json`) containing location data
- **No build tools**: This is a static site served directly

## Project Structure
```
Calm-Map/
├── index.html          # Main HTML file with embedded JavaScript
├── style.css           # All styling
├── points.json         # Location data (array of place objects)
├── docs/               # Image assets and documentation
└── README.md           # Project documentation
```

## Code Style Guidelines

### JavaScript
- Use modern ES6+ syntax (const/let, arrow functions, template literals)
- Prefer functional programming patterns where appropriate
- Use meaningful variable names (e.g., `placesData`, `filterMarkers`)
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Comment complex logic, especially map-related operations

### CSS
- Use semantic class names (e.g., `.popup-content`, `.place-item`)
- Follow BEM-like naming for components
- Keep styles organized by component/section
- Use CSS custom properties for colors if repeating values
- Mobile-first responsive design when applicable

### HTML
- Use semantic HTML5 elements
- Maintain accessibility (alt text for images, proper labels)
- Keep structure clean and readable

## Key Patterns

### Map Markers
- Markers are created from `points.json` data
- Each marker stores metadata as properties (`marker.name`, `marker.category`, etc.)
- Popups use HTML templates with place data
- Filtering removes/adds markers from map dynamically

### Data Structure
Places in `points.json` have this structure:
```json
{
  "name": "Place Name",
  "lat": 33.1234,
  "lng": -111.5678,
  "address": "Full address string",
  "vibe-type": "Nature|Eats & Drinks|Exercise|Wellness|Arts|Quiet",
  "cost": "Easy on the wallet|Something reasonable|Big treat to self",
  "accessibility": "Easy|Moderate|Challenging",
  "image": "URL to image"
}
```

### Filtering Logic
- Multiple filters work together (search, category, cost, accessibility)
- Filters are applied on user input events
- Both map markers and sidebar list update together
- "Show all" button resets all filters and zooms to bounds

## Common Tasks

### Adding a New Place
1. Add entry to `points.json` with all required fields
2. Ensure image URL is accessible
3. Test that marker appears and popup displays correctly

### Modifying Filters
- Filter logic is in `filterMarkers()` function
- Update filter UI in HTML sidebar section
- Ensure both marker and list filtering stay in sync

### Styling Changes
- Map popups: `.leaflet-popup-content-wrapper` and `.popup-*` classes
- Sidebar: `#sidebar` and child elements
- Responsive adjustments: modify `#map` width calculation

## Important Notes
- Map is initialized centered on Phoenix, AZ area
- Default location fallback is Istanbul, Turkey coordinates
- Location services are currently commented out
- All images are hosted on GitHub (raw URLs)
- No external dependencies beyond CDN links

## Documentation Maintenance

**CRITICAL**: When making code changes, always update relevant documentation:

- **If you modify code structure**: Update `.cursor/architecture.md` and project structure sections
- **If you add new patterns**: Update `.cursor/patterns.md` with examples
- **If you change workflows**: Update `.cursor/workflows.md`
- **If you add features**: Update `README.md` and relevant sections in `.cursorrules`
- **If you change data structures**: Update data structure documentation in `.cursorrules` and `.cursor/architecture.md`

Before completing any task, verify that documentation accurately reflects the current codebase state.

## When Making Changes
- Test in browser after changes
- Ensure markers display correctly
- Verify filters work as expected
- Check that popups render properly
- Maintain the calm, peaceful aesthetic of the design
- **Update relevant documentation** (see Documentation Maintenance section above)
